From 59b895c894451a3276a97c7f8e04fc3db594d788 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 17:03:23 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(state): refuse reopening a compression parent under a
 live turn lease [daveey]

reopen_session() on a session stamped end_reason='compression' clears the
stamp. The turn-lease key is the compression root, found by walking
parent_session_id while the parent is stamped compression, so every
descendant's key silently moved from the root to the child. A holder
admitted on the root key then read "missing lease row" on its next
transcript flush and a turn it validly owned was refused
(session_persistence_failed:turn_lease).

The reopen now runs the guard every other lease-less transcript mutation
runs: a live row refuses (retryable busy), a stale row is reclaimed, a
parked update turn refuses. It never inserts or migrates a row, so
released, never-acquired and superseded holders still refuse and the
absence-based fence is unchanged. Reopening a session that is not a
compression parent is unchanged.

Why: found by exhaustive depth-2 model check of acquire/refresh/flush/
release/rotate/reopen/end over the real SessionDB; the only
valid-owner-refused counterexample reachable from a compression lineage.
NOT attributed to Desktop incident 20000101_000000_00000b: that session
has no parent, so its key was its own id.

Proof: tests/hermes_state/test_reopen_compression_parent_turn_lease.py,
4 of 6 fail on origin/main (missing lease row for the valid owner), 6/6
pass; controls: released/never-acquired holder refuses without inserting
a row, stale row reclaimed, non-compression reopen unchanged, Event-ordered
two-connection reopen vs owner flush. Regression: 324 files / 4221 tests
(hermes_state, reopen callers in gateway/tui_gateway/cli) passed.

Hermes-Card: t_eeeeee000000002b
